# Verdict on the harness loop: Quad Relay channel 1 wired in series through
# Digital In 4 channel 1 and back to the lock PSU. Toggling the relay and
# watching the input follow proves that these two specific Bricklets are wired
# to each other and to a lock supply. Device discovery alone proves only that
# some Quad Relay and some Digital In 4 are plugged in, which any user with
# unrelated Bricklets would satisfy.
#
# Verified is what gets reported to the EVSE as "bricklet dedication verified".
# It is the arming signal for enabling the feature. Note the direction: anything
# other than Verified means the dedication could not be proven, not that the
# Bricklets are unrelated - a dead lock PSU produces Fail Open with both
# Bricklets still fitted. Only Bricklets Not Found says anything about whether
# the hardware is there.
#
# Challenging is the release phase in progress. The reported verdict is held
# across it, because the loop legitimately reads low while the relay is open.
#
# The three failures are kept apart because they mean different things, and
# conflating the first two is what made the disable gate unsound in the first
# place.
#
# Fail Open is a loop that will not close with the relay energized: a cut link,
# a dead lock supply, a loose DC connector. Both Bricklets are still there.
#
# Fail Stuck is a loop that stays high with the relay released - a welded
# contact, a reversed relay channel whose body diode conducts, or a strap tied
# across the input to fake the marker.
#
# Bricklets Not Found means discovery came up empty, so there is nothing to run
# a challenge against. Appended as 5 rather than inserted, so the existing
# values keep their meaning.
Unknown = 0
Challenging = 1
Verified = 2
Fail Open = 3
Fail Stuck = 4
Bricklets Not Found = 5
